course: religion-ethics
lesson: 103
title: Olympiad: fair or unfair, chains of reasons and dilemmas ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: "That's not fair!" is the most shouted sentence in any playground, and an olympiad asks you to test it: fair can mean equal shares, shares by need or shares by effort, and the right one depends on the situation.
explain: Think of a chain of reasons: "It's fair because it's the rule", "Why is the rule fair?", "Because the head said so", "Why?"; a good answer still stands after two or three "why?"s, and a chain is only as strong as its weakest link.
explain: Chains of events work the same way: if Ali grabs Ben's ball, Ben shoves Ali, and Ali falls on Cal's sandcastle, each person owes a sorry for their own link, and nobody's sorry wipes out someone else's.
explain: The Bible has fairness puzzles too: Solomon found the real mother of a baby by watching who loved the child more than winning (1 Kings 3), Zacchaeus paid back four times what he had cheated (Luke 19:8), and the vineyard owner paid late workers the same as early ones (Matthew 20).
explain: Watch the words: "everyone's allowed", "I NEVER get a go" and "it's only a bit unfair" are often hyperbole or downplaying, so check the real facts before you judge, and give reasons from rules, consequences and character.
fact: Fairness means no favourites; it can mean equal shares, shares by need, or shares by effort, and people disagree about which fits best in a given case.
fact: Equality gives everyone the same; equity gives each person what they need to have the same chance, like glasses for the child who cannot see the board.
fact: "One cuts, the other chooses" is fair because the cutter has every reason to make the two pieces equal.
fact: A fair rule is known to everyone before the game starts; changing it afterwards to suit one side is unfair.
fact: Fair ways to share something scarce include drawing lots, a waiting list and helping those in greatest need; choosing your friends is favouritism.
fact: In 1 Kings 3, two women claimed one baby; Solomon ordered it cut in two, and the real mother begged him to give the baby to the other woman rather than let it die, so he gave it to her.
fact: Zacchaeus, a tax collector, promised to give half his possessions to the poor and to pay back four times anyone he had cheated (Luke 19:8).
fact: In the parable of the workers in the vineyard, every worker received the one coin agreed, and the owner asked, "Are you envious because I am generous?" (Matthew 20:15); people still argue about what the story teaches.
fact: The Golden Rule appears in many traditions: Jesus said "Do to others what you would have them do to you" (Matthew 7:12), and the Jewish teacher Hillel said "What is hateful to you, do not do to your neighbour".
fact: "Do not follow the crowd in doing wrong" (Exodus 23:2): "everyone does it" is not a reason that something is fair.
fact: Rules thinking asks "what is my duty?", consequences thinking asks "what will happen to everyone?", and character thinking asks "what would a good person do?"
fact: Many people think it unfair to punish a whole class for what one person did, though teachers sometimes do it when nobody owns up; it is disputed.
fact: A strong olympiad answer gives reasons, faces the best argument on the other side, and treats the other team with respect.
fact: Slang check: "bare" (London) means lots of; "mardy" (Midlands, North) means sulky; "sus" means suspicious; "y'all" means you all; "no cap" means no lie.
exercise: Zacchaeus cheated three people out of 5 coins each. Paying back four times, how many coins does he hand back altogether? | 60 | 15 | 20 | 45
exercise: Same Zacchaeus: he owns 200 coins before paying anyone back and gives half to the poor first. How many coins are left for paying back? | 100 | 200 | 50 | 150
exercise: Ali grabs Ben's ball, Ben shoves Ali, and Ali lands on Cal's sandcastle. Who owes Cal a sorry? | Ben mostly, for the shove, though Ali started it by grabbing | Only Ali, because he is the one who landed on it | Only Ben, so Ali needs to say nothing to anybody | Nobody, because it was just an accident
exercise: In the same chain, what does Ali owe Ben? | A sorry for grabbing the ball | Nothing, because Ben shoved him | A new ball and a sandcastle | A shove back, to make it even
exercise: Why did Solomon's test with the sword show who the real mother was? | She would rather lose the baby than see it hurt | She was the one who cried the loudest in front of the king | She spoke first, so the king believed her story straight away | He knew her face already from an earlier trial at court
exercise: A team of four wins a £30 voucher. Split by effort, Mia did half the work and the other three did equal shares of the rest. What does each of the three get? | £5 | £10 | £7.50 | £15
exercise: Odd one out among these reasons for giving Sam the last place on the trip: "he needs it most", "he was first on the list", "we drew his name", "he's my best mate". Which, and why? | "He's my best mate", because it is favouritism | "He needs it most", as need is never fair | "We drew his name", because drawing lots is only luck | "He was first on the list", as lists are old
exercise: Which reason is a consequences reason for not cheating at a test? | The teacher would not know what you really need help with | Cheating is against the rules, full stop | A good person is honest even when nobody at all can see them | You promised your mum you never would
exercise: The race is over and the teacher says, "New rule: only kids in blue win." Why is that unfair? | The rule was not known before the race | Blue is not a fair colour for the winners to wear | There should never be any winners in a school race | Rules can never be changed at all, by anyone, ever
exercise: Kim says, "Same for everyone is ALWAYS fair." Which example shows she is wrong? | Giving every child size-3 shoes | Giving every child a turn on the swing | Giving every child one vote | Giving every child a copy of the rules
exercise: Which saying is the same idea as Jesus' "Do to others what you would have them do to you"? | Hillel's "What is hateful to you, do not do to your neighbour" | "Finders keepers, losers weepers" | "An eye for an eye, a tooth for a tooth, and no more said" | "Every person for themselves, and may the fastest win"
exercise: A worker hired at dawn agreed to one coin and got one coin; a worker hired at five got one coin too. Was the first one cheated? | No, he got what he agreed, though many feel it's unequal | Yes, the owner broke the promise he had made to him that morning | Yes, he should have been paid in silver | No, because he was paid in gold
exercise: A chain: "It's fair because it's the rule." "Why is the rule fair?" "Because I said so." Where is the weak link? | "Because I said so" gives no reason | "It's the rule" is always the end of it | Asking "why?" is rude, so the asker is the weak link | There is no weak link at all here
exercise: One medicine dose, two equally sick children, no other help. Which way of choosing do most people call fairest? | Drawing lots | Picking the one you like better | Picking the richer family | Picking the one with better marks
exercise: Two kids share one cake, but the same child both cuts AND chooses first. What has gone wrong? | The cutter no longer needs to cut fairly | Nothing, that's the classic fair method | The cake should be cut into three pieces | The younger child must always cut it
exercise: Rules, consequences, character: which question belongs to character thinking? | What would a good, kind person do here? | What does the school rule book say about it? | What will happen to everyone? | What do I get out of it?
test: "Everyone in my class is allowed to stay up till midnight, everyone!" moans Jay. What is he most likely really saying? | Some friends have said they stay up late | All thirty kids stay up till midnight every night | Nobody in his class goes to bed at all | His parents have said yes to midnight
test: Leo's well mardy: "I NEVER get a go on the console!" The rota shows he had 3 of the last 10 turns, with 4 kids sharing. Fair reply? | You had a bit more than your share, so "never" isn't true | Yeah, you never ever get a go on it, no cap, that is well unfair | You had 10 turns, so stop moaning | 3 out of 10 is nothing, so you should get the rest
test: One kid flicks paint and nobody owns up, so Miss keeps y'all in at break. A reasoned view? | Many feel it's unfair to punish everyone, though some say it pushes the guilty one to own up | It's always perfectly fair, end of | It's fair only if the class is very big | It's unfair because breaks are never allowed to be stopped
test: Your mate goes, "It's only a bit unfair, no biggie," but he's been left out of every game for a week. What is he really saying? | It hurts more than he says; include him, maybe tell a teacher | It's fine, he truly does not mind being left out at all, honest | He actually wants to be left out of the games for a bit longer | He thinks all those games are boring anyway, so it's no loss
test: "Zacchaeus paying back four times is bare extra, once is fair, innit," says Kofi. Best-reasoned reply? | Once is the least; four times shows real sorrow | He's right, paying back more than you took is always silly | Zacchaeus never paid anything back to anybody, he ran off | Four times is still the law for everyone in Britain today
test: "Solomon was sus, he was gonna chop a baby!" says Ava. What is a better reading? | It was a test; he never meant to, and it showed the real mother's love | She's right, he really did chop the baby in two in front of everyone at court | Solomon wanted the baby for himself | The baby was a doll, so it didn't matter
test: Dad: "Lowkey the vineyard boss was well unfair, the late lot did, like, an hour." A reasoned answer? | Many feel that way, but nobody got less than agreed, and the story is about generosity | He's right, the boss stole wages from every worker who came early in the day, which is a crime | The late workers worked longest of all | Everyone was paid nothing at the end
test: Team A at the olympiad says, "Team B is just wrong, they're silly." Why will judges mark A down? | They attacked the people instead of answering the best argument | They spoke too quietly | They agreed with Team B on everything | They gave too many good reasons
test: Grandad: "Back in my day we just picked teams, best first. Fair's fair." What's a fair reply? | Picking the best first leaves the same kids last; drawing names or numbering off is fairer | Yes, best first is the only fair way, because then the strongest players can win the match | Teams should be picked by height | The slowest should always be captain
test: "If it's not against the rules, it's fair, no cap," says Max, who sat on the ball so nobody else could play. What's the flaw? | Something can break no rule and still be unfair, like hogging the ball so others can't play | He's right: anything that is allowed by the rules is fair, so sitting on the ball is fine as well | Sitting on a ball is always against the rules | Fairness only matters in board games
test: Ruby: "You took my seat — no wait, I left it to get a drink, and now you're in it." What matters most for a fair answer? | Whether the seat was free or saved when she left | Who is taller | Who got to the seat first this morning, before the bell went at all | Who has the nicer bag
exam: Identify which of the following constitutes a reason grounded in need rather than in effort or equality. | The allocation of glasses to the child unable to see the board | The division of the prize into identical parts | The award of the greater part to the hardest worker | The selection of a recipient by the drawing of lots
exam: Ascertain why the procedure "one cuts, the other chooses" is regarded as fair. | The cutter has every reason to make the two parts equal | The elder child is entitled to the larger part | The chooser is required to accept the smaller part | The procedure leaves the outcome entirely to chance
exam: Select the statement most consistent with the account of Zacchaeus in Luke 19:8. | He undertook to repay fourfold any he had cheated and to give half his possessions to the poor | He repaid exactly what he had taken | He refused all restitution | He gave everything to the Temple
exam: Which response to the question "Is fair always equal?" would olympiad judges regard most highly? | One which distinguishes equality from need and effort, with examples, and weighs which fits the case | One which answers yes without reason | One which answers no without reason | One which declines to answer
